From f358c1c0a8abf381698dc5d0f8a12e56c300b39e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chen Jun <newdays-cj@live.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 15:55:37 +0800
Subject: [PATCH v2 4/8] mm/hugetlb: pre-optimize arm64 boot gigantic pages

Avoid allocating the full vmemmap backing for boot-configured arm64
1 GiB HugeTLB pages with 4 KiB base pages. Capture HugeTLB arguments early
and replay them after node state, zone limits and reliable-memory policy
are ready, before ordinary struct page initialization.

Defer sparse backing while retaining section usage metadata. Preserve
DMA CMA, crashkernel, explicit memmap and folio CMA reservation priority,
constrain gigantic allocations to a node and zone, and protect ordinary
memory with a conservative metadata budget. Skip aliased tail writes and
hand optimized pages to the existing restore and demotion paths.

Use the existing hugetlb_free_vmemmap setting. Keep the previous boot
path when HVO is disabled or HugeTLB CMA is active. Add isolated QEMU
selftests for data integrity, compound flags, shrinking, demotion, NUMA
placement and deliberately rejected requests without host pool changes.

The pre-HVO design follows the community boot-time backing-avoidance
approach, adapted to this kernel's initialization and memory policies.
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20250228182928.2645936-1-fvdl@google.com

QEMU reports 4095 avoided backing pages per optimized 1 GiB page. This
excludes extra page tables and does not claim whole-system peak savings.

Signed-off-by: Chen Jun <newdays-cj@live.com>
Co-authored-by: sisyphus-dev-ai <sisyphus-dev-ai@users.noreply.github.com>
